## Proof

**Claim:** Not every symmetric matrix can be decomposed into a sum of two positive definite matrices. The answer is **No**.

**Key fact:** The sum of two positive definite matrices is positive definite.

*Proof of key fact.* Let $P, Q$ be positive definite. For any nonzero vector $v$,
$$v^{\top}(P+Q)v = v^{\top}Pv + v^{\top}Qv > 0 + 0 = 0,$$
since each term is strictly positive by positive definiteness. Hence $P+Q$ is positive definite. $\square$

**Counterexample.** Consider the symmetric matrix $A = -I$ (the negative identity matrix, in any dimension $n \ge 1$). Its eigenvalues are all $-1 < 0$, so $A$ is **not** positive definite — in fact it is negative definite.

Suppose for contradiction that $A = P + Q$ with $P, Q$ positive definite. By the key fact, $P + Q$ is positive definite, so $A = -I$ would have to be positive definite. But for any unit vector $e_1$,
$$e_1^{\top}(-I)e_1 = -1 < 0,$$
contradicting positive definiteness. Therefore no such decomposition exists for $A = -I$.

**Conclusion.** Since there exists a symmetric matrix (e.g., $-I$) that cannot be written as a sum of two positive definite matrices, it is **not** the case that *any* symmetric matrix admits such a decomposition. In fact, a necessary condition is that the matrix itself be positive definite (since the sum of two positive definite matrices is positive definite), so the class of decomposable symmetric matrices is exactly the positive definite ones — a strict subset of all symmetric matrices.

